00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:19.500
the information in their own domains. And
because of this enormous tsunami of information

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:26.260
generated by, um, uh, the digital universe,
uh, that semi monopoly of information has

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:31.090
been lost. And the second that happened, these
institutions, including government, they could

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:37.160
in politics and political parties say a half
laps into what I've called the crisis of authority.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:41.290
It seems that this is a double edged sword
though in the sense that on the one hand the

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:47.440
internet has greatly democratized reporting,
information, availability, diversity of sources,

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:54.280
so on and so forth. And at the same time,
this has been a catalyst for the crisis of

00:02:54.280 --> 00:03:00.230
fake news and disinformation, the inability
to distinguish fact fiction, opinion and reporting,

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:04.770
so on and so forth. How, what's your sense
of the balance of those two forces?

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:10.820
Well, I mean I think there is no balance.
I think we are in a totally fractured environment.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:17.090
When you have a crisis of authority, you are
bound to get a crisis of uncertainty. Okay.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:25.370
So, um, you people live in what I call the
public. Uh, it, I don't mean by that, that

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:32.000
people, I don't mean by that the masses, the
public is essentially the old mass audience.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.981
Um, which in my day when I was younger, it
was like an enormous mirror, uh, that you

00:03:37.981 --> 00:03:42.480
saw yourself reflected in. Well, that mirror
has shattered as fallen. It has and the public

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:48.560
